### LangSmith RoboGrammar RobotDesignHitlV2 eval contract

- Date updated: 2026-06-01 local workspace session.
- Dataset `robogrammar-structural-rules-evals` now stores all 701 reference
  outputs as RobotDesignHitlV2 objects with top-level keys `version`, `spec`,
  `status`, `design_program`, `derivation_trace`,
  `expanded_graph_summary`, `validation`, `repair_history`,
  `component_program`, `compile_safe`, `loop_trace`, and `messages`.
  Legacy `expected_behavior`, `expected_compile_safe`, and
  `expected_invalid_nodes` remain in example metadata for filtering and
  behavior scoring.
- Shared helpers live in `packages/research/agent_loops/langsmith_output.py`; eval-side compatibility imports are in `evals/langsmith_contract.py`.
- `evals/run_langsmith_model_experiments.py` and `evals/run_langsmith_loop_experiments.py` return only the RobotDesignHitlV2 LangSmith object from targets and use deterministic `hallucination` and `quality` evaluators from `evals/run_langsmith_deterministic_evals.py`. The former standalone deterministic `alignment` evaluator remains retired; V2 quality now scores schema validity, absence of `structural_rules`, allowed V2 rule IDs, expected behavior vs. `compile_safe`/`status`, program shape, derivation trace, graph summary, component lowering, and task intent/embodiment alignment.
- Metric reporting is canonicalized to `mean_hallucination` and `mean_quality`.
  Older LangSmith feedback keys `alignment`, `task_alignment`, and
  `compiler_validity` are cleanup/audit signals only; summary and aggregation
  code must not emit mean metric columns for them or use them as fallbacks.
- Both LangSmith experiment runners also attach the default `nvidia_minimax_llm_judge_evaluator` from `evals/run_langsmith_llm_judge.py`, which emits distinct `llm_hallucination` and `llm_task_alignment` feedback against each example's RobotDesignHitlV2 reference output. The same module can backfill existing projects with `python -m evals.run_langsmith_llm_judge --project <project> [--limit N] [--dry-run]`.
- The default hosted judge endpoint is NVIDIA Integrate `https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1` with model `minimaxai/minimax-m2.7`; override through `LANGSMITH_EVAL_LLM_*` or `NVIDIA_MINIMAX_*` env vars, and use `--no-llm-judge` only for explicit cost/debug skips. `minimaxai/minimax-m2.5` returned HTTP 410 on 2026-05-27 because NVIDIA marked it EOL on 2026-05-12.
- UI-visible LangSmith workspace evaluator resources are registered through `python -m evals.register_langsmith_evaluators`. This calls `/v1/platform/evaluators`, not the feedback-config SDK path, and keeps the Evaluators page covering `hallucination`, `quality`, `llm_hallucination`, and `llm_task_alignment`. It skips keys already covered by an existing workspace evaluator unless `--include-covered` is passed.
- The notebook compiler-centric and compiler-feedback loops in `packages/research/notebooks/rl_playground.ipynb` still keep full HITL state locally, but their LangSmith observation output is `final_state["hitl"]["langsmith_output"]`.
- Historical LangSmith projects created before the V2 output contract may
  have legacy or empty target run outputs. Treat those as invalid for current
  comparison design; reruns through the updated scripts emit the V2 object
  contract and the reduced metric set.
- 2026-05-31 cleanup: the old RoboGrammar LangSmith experiment projects were
  removed from the workspace because sampled targets were legacy HITL fields,
  empty, or `{"output": null}` rather than the object-output contract. Current
  valid runs must be newly created after this cleanup. Do not treat local
  `evals/results/*.json` files that reference deleted historical project names
  as live LangSmith evidence.
- Current experiment-design guardrail: a loop comparison is valid only when
  each compared loop runs against the same dataset slice, provider, model,
  attempt limit, and evaluator set. Smoke runs and interrupted grammar-only
  runs are wiring evidence, not side-by-side performance evidence.

## Invariants
- Every scaffold revision is traceable to the rollout batch that motivated it; a revision with no
  antecedent evidence is rejected at write time.
- Every run is replayable from `(schema digest, task, seed, prompt version, model id)`.
- Run history is append-only; a rerun gets a new run id, never an overwrite.
- The negative control runs in every experiment; if it passes any of G1–G3, all results from that
  gate implementation are inadmissible.
